<Defs>

	<ThingDef ParentName="CGF1_MWP_AssaultRifle_T0">
		<defName>CGF1_MWP_Type95_Proposal_I_</defName>
		<label>Type 95 Proposal I</label>
		<description>Caliber: 5.8x42mm. 30-round detachable mag. Select-Fire.
		
This rifle is one of numerous designs that were submitted to the PLA during the early 1990s trials to adopt a new standard infantry arm. The PLA had experimented with a lighter intermediate cartridge in the 5.8x42mm round since the 1980s, developed with a similar mentality to the American 5.56x45mm and Soviet 5.45x39mm cartridges, but was still largely reliant on the 7.62x39-caliber Type 81. The potential replacement would use this new caliber, and additionally, it was evidently desired that the new rifle would be a bullpup (as the final production QBZ-95 ended up being.) This rifle is an example of one proposal that was submitted but ultimately rejected, and is especially unusual as it is the only known submission that was not a bullpup, but rather laid out similarly to the Type 81/87 rifles.</description>
